Z||000000||DaveL||06-18-2001||10:09 PM||||OK, getting ready to put a new exhaust manifold on my 87 2.5. I don't want to go with a used manifold, but I have a friend that thinks I'm crazy to spend the extra money. Anyway, the Hayne's Manual shows a particular sequence in which to tighten/torque the bolts. Why is that? This must sound like a stupid question. <BR>Also, this is the second one I will have put on. Why are they so prone to failure? It does look as though water from the decklid would puddle in the center of it, but the leak isn't from that spot. When I put my hand in the area near the O2 sensor, I can really feel the exhaust. I'm getting error codes whenever I get up to highway speed and the little tick the engine used to have sounds like a volkswagon without a muffler!!! The only noise in the car that was louder than that was my ex-wife when she was in it.<P>P.S. I fixed that one||||reg||5
Z||000001||85Josh||06-18-2001||10:19 PM||||The specific tightening pattern is to ensure that you evenly tighten the flange without warping it. I think the reason that they crack is because they are thin tubes, rather than heavy castiron, and our cars get really hot.<P>WooHoo! 300 Posts!<BR>Edited for spelling<P>------------------<BR>85 2M4(Lil' Bit)<p>[This message has been edited by 85Josh (edited 06-18-2001).]||||reg||1
